PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       These functions are described in The Single Unix Specification, Version
       2.  No error conditions are defined.

       This implementation returns EERRRR if the window pointer is  null,  or  if
       the lower-level wwggeettcchh(3X) call returns an EERRRR.

       SVr3  and  early  SVr4  curses  implementations did not reject function
       keys; the SVr4.0 documentation claimed that  “special  keys”  (such  as
       function  keys, “home” key, “clear” key, _e_t_c.) are “interpreted”, with‐
       out giving details.  It lied.  In fact, the “character” value  appended
       to  the  string by those implementations was predictable but not useful
       (being, in fact, the low-order eight bits of the key's KEY_ value).

       The functions ggeettnnssttrr, mmvvggeettnnssttrr, and mmvvwwggeettnnssttrr were present  but  not
       documented in SVr4.

       X/Open Curses, Issue 5 (2007) stated that these functions “read at most
       _n bytes” but did not state whether the terminating NUL  is  counted  in
       that  limit.   X/Open  Curses,  Issue 7 (2009) changed that to say they
       “read at most _n-1 bytes” to allow for the terminating NUL.  As of 2018,
       some implementations count it, some do not:

       •   _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  6.1  and PDCurses do not count the NUL in the given limit,
           while

       •   Solaris SVr4 and NetBSD curses count the NUL as part of the limit.

       •   Solaris xcurses provides both:  its  wide-character  wwggeett__nnssttrr  re‐
           serves a NUL, but its wwggeettnnssttrr does not count the NUL consistently.

       In SVr4 curses, a negative value of _n tells wwggeettnnssttrr to assume that the
       caller's buffer is large enough to hold the result, i.e., to  act  like
       wwggeettssttrr.   X/Open  Curses does not mention this (or anything related to
       negative or zero values of _n), however  most  implementations  use  the
       feature, with different limits:

       •   Solaris  SVr4  curses  and  PDCurses limit the result to 255 bytes.
           Other Unix systems than Solaris are likely to use the same limit.

       •   Solaris xcurses limits the result to LLIINNEE__MMAAXX bytes.

       •   NetBSD 7 assumes no particular limit for the result  from  wwggeettssttrr.
           However,  it  limits  the wwggeettnnssttrr parameter _n to ensure that it is
           greater than zero.

           A comment in NetBSD's source code states that this is specified  in
           SUSv2.

       •   _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  (before  6.2)  assumes  no particular limit for the result
           from wwggeettssttrr, and treats the _n  parameter  of  wwggeettnnssttrr  like  SVr4
           curses.

       •   _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  6.2  uses  LLIINNEE__MMAAXX,  or a larger (system-dependent) value
           which the ssyyssccoonnff function may provide.   If  neither  LLIINNEE__MMAAXX  or
           ssyyssccoonnff  is available, _n_c_u_r_s_e_s uses the POSIX value for LLIINNEE__MMAAXX (a
           2048 byte limit).  In either case, it reserves a byte for the  ter‐
           minating NUL.

       Although  ggeettnnssttrr  is equivalent to a series of calls to ggeettcchh, it also
       makes changes to the curses modes to allow simple editing of the  input
       buffer:

       •   ggeettnnssttrr  saves  the  current  value of the nnll, eecchhoo, rraaww and ccbbrreeaakk
           modes, and sets nnll, nnooeecchhoo, nnoorraaww, and ccbbrreeaakk.

           ggeettnnssttrr handles the echoing of characters, rather than  relying  on
           the caller to set an appropriate mode.

       •   It  also  obtains  the _e_r_a_s_e and _k_i_l_l characters from eerraasseecchhaarr and
           kkiillllcchhaarr, respectively.

       •   On return, ggeettnnssttrr restores the modes to their previous values.

       Other implementations differ in their treatment of special characters:

       •   While they may set the _e_c_h_o mode, other implementations do not mod‐
           ify  the  _r_a_w mode, They may take the _c_b_r_e_a_k mode set by the caller
           into account when deciding whether to handle echoing within ggeettnnssttrr
           or as a side-effect of the ggeettcchh calls.

       •   The original _n_c_u_r_s_e_s (as _p_c_u_r_s_e_s in 1986) set nnoorraaww and ccbbrreeaakk when
           accepting input for ggeettnnssttrr.  That may have been done to make func‐
           tion- and cursor-keys work; it is not necessary with _n_c_u_r_s_e_s.

           Since  1995, _n_c_u_r_s_e_s has provided signal handlers for INTR and QUIT
           (e.g., ^^CC or ^^\\).  With the nnoorraaww and ccbbrreeaakk  settings,  those  may
           catch  a  signal  and stop the program, where other implementations
           allow one to enter those characters in the buffer.

       •   Starting in 2021 (_n_c_u_r_s_e_s 6.3), ggeettnnssttrr sets rraaww, rather than nnoorraaww
           and  ccbbrreeaakk for better compatibility with SVr4-curses, e.g., allow‐
           ing one to enter a ^^CC into the buffer.

